  <h2>What is firing sequence?</h2>

  <p>
    A powerful swing isn't created because every body part moves.
  </p>

  <p>
    It's created because every body part moves at the right time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Just like an assembly line, every step depends on the one before it.
    If one station works too early or too late, the entire process becomes
    less efficient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The swing works the same way.
  </p>

  <p>
    After loading into a balanced position, the hitter is finally ready to
    begin transferring energy toward the baseball.
  </p>

  <p>
    The front side stabilizes, bringing the body's forward movement under
    control.
  </p>

  <p>
    That stability allows the lower half to begin rotating.
  </p>

  <p>
    As the hips rotate, energy is transferred into the torso.
  </p>

  <p>
    The torso continues that transfer into the upper body.
  </p>

  <p class="emphasis">
    Every movement prepares the one that follows.
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, once the decision to swing has been made, the hands fire toward
    the contact position.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sequence is what allows the body to work as one connected system
    instead of a collection of independent parts.
  </p>

  <p class="final-thought">
    The power isn't in one body part.
    <br />
    It's in how they work together.
  </p>
</ChapterLayout>
